    <string name="feature_settings_circuit_fewer">Only %1$d of %2$d hops available: not enough operators run relaying nodes</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_circuit_count">Circuits: %1$d</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_circuit_count_hint">Your contacts are spread across the circuits, each ending at a node of a different operator when there are enough, so no single operator sees all of them. Each circuit uses a little more battery and data.</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_circuit_count_open">%1$d of %2$d circuits open</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_decoy_count">Decoy envelopes: %1$d per send</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_decoy_count_off">Decoy envelopes: off</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_decoy_count_hint">With each message, this many decoys go into other contacts' mailboxes at the same moment, so a mailbox node cannot tell from timing whom you wrote to or who is in a group. They cost a little data for you and the contacts that get them; messages are not delayed. Sent only through a protecting circuit.</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_circuit_operators_fewer">Your circuits end at only %1$d of %2$d different operators: not enough operators run relaying nodes</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_circuit_send_directly">Send directly without a route</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_circuit_send_directly_on">While no route through at least two nodes is available, sending and receiving through mailboxes go directly or through a single node: messages keep working, but a server sees your address.</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_circuit_send_directly_off">Sending and receiving through mailboxes wait for a route through at least two nodes: your address stays hidden, but with fewer nodes reachable, messages wait until enough are back.</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_preferred_node_automatic">Automatic</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_preferred_node_automatic_description">Uses a node picked for this device</string>
    <string name="feature_settings_preferred_node_hint">Your favourite node is used first. If it is unreachable, Sparrow switches to another node automatically and returns once it is back. Whoever runs the node you connect through can see your IP address; your messages stay end-to-end encrypted.</string>
